32. The complete navigation concept

The most important thing to understand is the navigation stack.

Initially:

┌──────────────┐
│     Home     │
└──────────────┘

User clicks Go to About:

┌──────────────┐
│    About     │ ← current page
├──────────────┤
│     Home     │
└──────────────┘

This happens because:

Navigator.pushNamed(context, '/about');

Then the user clicks Back:

Navigator.pop(context);

Flutter removes About:

┌──────────────┐
│     Home     │ ← current page
└──────────────┘
33. The three most important lines

For named routes, remember these three concepts:

Define routes
routes: {
  '/': (context) => const HomePage(),
  '/about': (context) => const AboutPage(),
  '/contact': (context) => const ContactPage(),
},
Go to another page
Navigator.pushNamed(context, '/about');
Go back
Navigator.pop(context);

So the basic pattern is:

Define route
     ↓
pushNamed()
     ↓
New screen
     ↓
pop()
     ↓
Previous screen

One important distinction: with normal Navigator.push(), you directly specify the widget/page to open. With named routes, you first give each page a route name and then navigate using that name. This becomes especially useful when your Flutter application has many screens.